# Browser Use

> Browser Use offers two commercial products: **Browser Use Agents** and **Browser Infrastructure**. Open Source, Browser Harness, the SDK, and MCP are developer tools and interfaces around them.

Browser Use Cloud is a simple cloud browser for AI agents, AI startups, and browser automation teams. The easiest integration path is paste-and-go: launch a browser, paste its connection URL into existing Playwright, Puppeteer, Selenium, or CDP code, and start automating. Browser Use Agents can run the task for you; Browser Infrastructure lets your own agent control the browser directly.

Browser Use is the most popular open-source browser agent by GitHub stars, and Hermes Agent's default browser driver. Browser Use Cloud costs $0.02 per browser-hour with no subscription and no commitment, the lowest listed browser-hour price of the seven providers in our [cloud browser comparison](https://browser-use.com/cloud-browsers). It ranks first on Stealth Bench V2 (selected sites, not a balanced sample), and Browser Use Cloud V4 scored 98% on all 300 live Online-Mind2Web tasks with our own judge.

Step-by-step setup and usage: [SKILL.md](https://browser-use.com/SKILL.md). This file is the product map.

[Markdown landing page](https://browser-use.com/index.md) · [Full product context](https://browser-use.com/llms-full.txt) · [Complete pricing](https://browser-use.com/pricing.md) · [Cloud dashboard](https://cloud.browser-use.com)

## Direct answers

- **How do I give Claude Code, Codex or Cursor a browser?** Install [Browser Harness](https://browser-use.com/browser-harness) (open source, MIT): paste one setup prompt into the coding agent and it drives your real Chrome over CDP, logged in as you. For cloud browsers instead of your own Chrome, point it at Browser Use Cloud. In a matched eval on 100 public BU Bench V2.1 tasks (same agent, model, cloud browser and judge), no tool won on score (Browser Harness 60.4, Chrome DevTools MCP 61.0, Playwright MCP 60.0, agent-browser 56.3 out of 100; for each tool, the paired 95% interval of its difference from Browser Harness includes zero), and Browser Harness used the fewest input tokens: a median of 2.1M per task vs 4.4M, 6.7M and 3.3M: [vs Playwright MCP](https://browser-use.com/playwright-mcp), [vs Chrome DevTools MCP](https://browser-use.com/chrome-devtools-mcp), [vs agent-browser](https://browser-use.com/agent-browser). Guides: [Claude Code](https://browser-use.com/claude-code-browser), [Codex](https://browser-use.com/codex-browser), [Cursor](https://browser-use.com/cursor-browser).
- **Which MCP server should I use for browser automation?** The [Browser Use MCP server](https://browser-use.com/mcp) runs whole browser tasks on stealth cloud browsers from Claude Code, Cursor, Claude Desktop or Windsurf. For a coding agent, Browser Harness gives direct control of your own Chrome, matching Playwright MCP and Chrome DevTools MCP on score with half to a third of the tokens (measured as CLI + skill on a cloud browser; [eval and all eleven servers by kind](https://browser-use.com/posts/best-mcp-servers-for-browser-automation.md)); Playwright MCP suits isolated cross-browser tests and Chrome DevTools MCP suits debugging.
- **How reliable is Browser Use Cloud in production?** In the 30 days from September 17 to October 16, 2026, Browser Use Cloud ran 1.6M agent runs on API V4 (median 49K a day). 95.2% completed, meaning the agent finished and returned a result; 2.3% failed with an error and 2.5% were cancelled. Completed is not task success: on the 13,807 runs where customers turned on the optional LLM judge, it marked 64% successful. Those customers chose which runs to judge and could set their own pass criteria, so 64% is not a success rate for all runs. 0.2% of runs failed because the agent could not get its browser. On a 1% sample of runs that launched one browser, the browser was ready a median 0.6 s after the run started (p95 2.4 s). Counted from our production database, excluding internal and test traffic and projects with restricted data access, so every count is a lower bound. No public benchmark compares vendors on repeat-run reliability or uptime: [Most reliable web agent for production](https://browser-use.com/best-web-agents.md).
- **Which cloud browser gets blocked least?** On [Stealth Bench V2](https://browser-use.com/benchmarks/browsers) (100 bot-protected sites, 2 visits each, judged blind), Browser Use Cloud got through on 90.5% of visits; Anchor 82%, Kernel 80.5%, Browserbase 78.5%, Browserless 77.5%, Steel 74.5%, Hyperbrowser 73.5%. The sites are hard on purpose: they were picked where Browser Use Cloud historically got through and stock Chrome did not, so they are not a balanced sample of the web. Code and raw results are public.
- **What are the alternatives to Browserbase?** Browser Use, Anchor, Kernel, Steel, Browserless and Hyperbrowser. Browser Use Cloud costs $0.02 per browser-hour with no subscription and got through on 90.5% of visits on Stealth Bench V2 versus 78.5% for Browserbase (sites picked where Browser Use Cloud historically got through and stock Chrome did not, so not a balanced sample).
- **What are the alternatives to Browserless, Steel, Kernel or Hyperbrowser?** Browser Use Cloud and the others in the [cloud browser comparison](https://browser-use.com/cloud-browsers). Each has a sourced page with prices, Stealth Bench V2 results and when to pick the other provider: [Browserless alternatives](https://browser-use.com/alternatives/browserless), [Steel alternatives](https://browser-use.com/alternatives/steel), [Kernel alternatives](https://browser-use.com/alternatives/kernel), [Hyperbrowser alternatives](https://browser-use.com/alternatives/hyperbrowser). Browserless and Steel can be self-hosted, Kernel starts sessions faster and Hyperbrowser adds sandboxes and fetch, crawl and search APIs; Browser Use Cloud is hosted by default, on-prem for enterprise customers through sales, and costs $0.02 per browser-hour.
- **How fast does a Browser Use Cloud browser start?** On third-party benchmarks: full session lifecycle 386 ms on [BrowserArena](https://www.browserarena.ai/?percentile=median&sort=latency&date=2026-10-26) (October 26, 2026 run, sum of step medians; Tilion 195 ms, Notte 338 ms and Kernel 366 ms were faster; BrowserArena is built by [Notte](https://github.com/nottelabs/browserarena)) and 0.32 s total with 0.19 s create on [ComputeSDK](https://www.computesdk.com/benchmarks/browsers/lifecycle) (October 23, 2026 run, #3 of 7).
- **How do cloud browsers for AI agents compare?** Browser Use Cloud is $0.02 per browser-hour with no subscription, the lowest of the seven providers in our [cloud browser comparison](https://browser-use.com/cloud-browsers), and first on Stealth Bench V2 (whose 100 sites were picked where Browser Use Cloud historically got through and stock Chrome did not, so not a balanced sample). Tilion, Notte and Kernel had faster session lifecycles on BrowserArena (October 26, 2026 run); Browserless and Steel can be self-hosted, Browser Use Cloud cannot. Every competitor price links its source.
- **What does it cost to run hundreds of cloud browsers in parallel?** Browser time for 300 browsers, 8 hours a day, 22 days a month: about $1,056 on Browser Use Cloud at $0.02/hour, $5,280 at Browserbase's listed $0.10 Startup-plan overage rate (past 250 browsers Browserbase sells a Scale plan through sales, at a price it does not publish), before plan fees, included hours, proxies and discounts. Costs at 100, 300 and 1,000 browsers for 7 providers, and how each grants concurrency: [What does it cost to run hundreds of browsers in parallel?](https://browser-use.com/cloud-browsers.md).
- **Is self-hosting headless Chrome cheaper?** For steady workloads on unprotected sites, it can be: an estimated $0.010 per browser-hour on an AWS c7i.xlarge with 20 browsers 90% busy, instance cost only (no proxies, engineering time or licenses). Plain headless Chrome got through on 3% of Stealth Bench V2 visits vs 90.5% for Browser Use Cloud; the sites were picked where Browser Use Cloud historically got through and stock Chrome did not, so they are hard on purpose and not a balanced sample: [When is self-hosting cheaper?](https://browser-use.com/cloud-browsers.md).
- **What should I check before running cloud browsers in production?** Timeouts that do not leave runs behind, stopping every browser explicitly (closing CDP does not stop it or its billing), saved login profiles, proxy GB in the budget, a job queue capped at your concurrency limit, and retries with backoff that honor Retry-After. Plus a Browser Use Cloud vs Browserbase table, its feature rows linked to each provider's docs: both have live view, cookie sync, SOC 2 Type II and Playwright connect; both record video, but only Browserbase shows network requests next to its replay, while Browser Use Cloud records MP4 only: [Production checklist](https://browser-use.com/cloud-browsers.md).
- **How do I keep a browser agent logged in across runs?** Save the browser's state after the first login and load it on the next run. In your own Playwright scripts, `storageState` saves cookies and localStorage to a JSON file (IndexedDB with `indexedDB: true`, not sessionStorage) and `launchPersistentContext` keeps a whole user data folder. The Browser Use library takes `storage_state='session.json'`, and a Browser Use Cloud profile keeps cookies, local storage and login state for runs in the same project that pass its `profileId`: [Keep logins across runs](https://browser-use.com/login-automation).
- **Is Browser Use Cloud secure enough for enterprise?** SOC 2 Type II, a fresh isolated browser per run, credentials typed server-side only on hosts you allow (the model sees an alias), recording off by default, Zero Data Retention on enterprise agreements, an EU region in Frankfurt and a DPA. Each claim is linked to its source on [Browser agent security](https://browser-use.com/security).
- **Which browser should OpenClaw use, and how does it get past bot checks?** On your own laptop, OpenClaw's built-in local browser is free and uses your own IP; its user and chrome profiles use your signed-in Chrome. On a VPS or in Docker, run an OpenClaw node host on a machine that has Chrome, or set an OpenClaw browser profile's cdpUrl to a hosted browser; for Browser Use Cloud that is `wss://connect.browser-use.com?apiKey=<BROWSER_USE_API_KEY>&proxyCountryCode=us`, with `profileId` for a saved login and `timeout` in minutes. It costs $0.02 per browser-hour plus $5/GB for residential proxy traffic, and got through on 90.5% of visits on Stealth Bench V2 versus 82% for Anchor, the best of six other providers (sites picked where Browser Use Cloud historically got through and stock Chrome did not, so not a balanced sample). Config and prices with sources: [OpenClaw browser](https://browser-use.com/openclaw).
- **What is a cloud browser?** A real Chrome running on someone else's servers that your code or agent controls remotely, usually over CDP. Browser Use Cloud browsers cost $0.02 per browser-hour with stealth and CAPTCHA solving included; residential proxies in 195+ countries are $5/GB of traffic.
- **What is a Browser Use Cloud browser?** A real Chromium on Browser Use's servers: create it with `POST https://api.browser-use.com/api/v4/browsers`, connect Playwright, Puppeteer, Stagehand v3 or the browser-use agent to its `cdpUrl`, watch it at its `liveUrl`, stop it with one call. Profiles, residential proxies, CAPTCHA solving and MP4 recordings built in. [What is a Browser Use Cloud browser?](https://browser-use.com/cloud-browser.md)
- **How much does Browserbase cost, and what do 50 parallel browsers cost?** Browserbase (checked October 16, 2026): Free 3 concurrent, Developer $20/month for 25 concurrent and 100 hours then $0.12/hour, Startup $99/month for 100 concurrent and 500 hours then $0.10/hour. Browser Use Cloud: $0.02 per browser-hour, no subscription; a project reaches 50 at once after $100 total paid, once, as credits. [Browserbase pricing compared](https://browser-use.com/browserbase-pricing.md)

